From b0e9f7605b16b1d305da6f33196885c4a35cb3d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 10:36:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Shard the style engine's memo caches and memoize the cascade
 projection
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Multithreading roadmap item #12, step 2 ("shard the caches"), plus the store its
step 3 needs.

`_cache`, `_sparseCache` and `_declaredCascadeCache` sat behind one `_sync`, so
every cache probe on the cascade's hot path was a Monitor acquire — tolerable
while one thread cascades, and the whole cost of the exercise once N threads do,
because a probe is the shortest possible critical section and therefore the one
with the worst work-to-synchronisation ratio. They are ConcurrentDictionary now.

What deliberately did not change is the generation-guard protocol. A cascade
still captures _cacheGeneration up front and still publishes under _sync, so
"is this result still current" and "publish it" cannot be split by a concurrent
InvalidateAll; InvalidateAll still clears inside the lock for the same reason,
even though the caches would no longer need it for their own sake. That guard is
what makes the lock-free compute window correct, so it survives by intent rather
than by omission.

The fourth cache is new. `GetCascadedStyle` memoized nothing at its own level —
only its inner declared cascade did — so everything between the two (custom
properties and var(), CSS-wide keywords, shorthand expansion, attr()
substitution, relative font weights) was recomputed on every call. Within one
render that saves nothing, because a render asks for each element once. It
exists so the renderer's warm pass has somewhere to put results: resolve every
element's cascade on N threads, then let the ordered box walk read them.

The concurrency test grows a second case. The existing one says the caches
survive concurrent access; it never compares a value, so it cannot say the
answers do. The new one takes a sequential cascade over one document and an
eight-thread cascade over a second, structurally identical one, and compares
property by property.
